automaticruby Repository Version History
========================================

v26.08 (Release Date: TBD)
--------------------------
- Raise the minimum Ruby to 3.2 and test on 3.2 through 3.4, replacing the "after Ruby 1.9" claim.
- Replace Kernel#open with URI.open throughout, so fetching a URL works on Ruby 3, and read local files with File.open.
- Replace File.exists? with File.exist? in the plugin loader and the Bundler setup.
- Load Recipes with YAML.safe_load, so a Recipe cannot name a Ruby class to instantiate; aliases stay permitted.
- Refuse a Recipe that is not a mapping or carries no plugins sequence, with the new Automatic::InvalidRecipeError.
- Give the framework an Automatic::Error base class, which NoRecipeError, NoPluginError and InvalidRecipeError derive from.
- Accept both a String and a Symbol as a log level, which plugins have always passed, and treat an unknown level as info instead of raising mid-run.
- Move the command line out of bin/automatic into Automatic::CLI, which returns an exit status and never calls exit, so it can be tested.
- Add -v and --version, and make --help and --version exit 0 instead of 1; a failed run exits 1 and a rejected command line exits 2.
- Report a missing or unreadable Recipe as one line on standard error instead of a backtrace.
- Load a subcommand's libraries inside that subcommand, so feedbag and the OPML parser are loaded only by the subcommands that use them.
- Stop scaffold creating ~/.automatic/plugins/. and ~/.automatic/plugins/.. from the directory listing.
- Stop the framework calling Bundler.require, which imposed this repository's bundle on any program requiring the library; the Gemfile setup now applies to a source checkout only.
- Require each plugin's own libraries in its own file, and keep gems used by one plugin out of the gem's runtime dependencies.
- Require aws-sdk inside the S3 branch of StoreFile, so downloading over HTTP no longer needs the AWS SDK installed.
- Require the plugin-internal files of the store and ChanToru plugins relatively, instead of through a load path entry.
- Call Sanitize.fragment in FilterSanitize, replacing Sanitize.clean, which the sanitize gem removed.
- Parse the fulltext siteinfo with JSON.parse over File.read, replacing JSON.load over an implicit Kernel#open.
- Verify TLS certificates in PublishInstapaper, which disabled verification outright.
- Stop PublishConsole and PublishConsoleLink printing a stray "info" line before each item, a leftover from the logging signature.
- Narrow the ActiveSupport requires of the pipeline, NotifyIkachan and SubscriptionXml to the extensions they use.
- Drop the unused NKF require from FilterGoogleNews, and declare nkf, which stopped being a default gem in Ruby 3.4.
- Replace the Jeweler-generated gemspec with a hand-maintained one, stating required_ruby_version, the licence, the homepage, the source URL and the metadata links, and deriving files from Git.
- Stop shipping the Gemfile and the Rakefile in the gem, so an installed copy no longer looks like a source checkout to environment.rb.
- Move the gem source to https://rubygems.org and drop gems.github.com, which has not existed since 2014.
- Split the dependencies into framework runtime gems, optional plugin gems and development gems, and drop the pins from 2015.
- Remove the dependencies on gcalapi, weather_hacker, twitter, pocket-ruby, hipchat, faraday, json and xml-simple, which the framework does not use and which belong to unsupported or optional plugins.
- Remove the development dependencies on jeweler, rcov, simplecov-rcov, cucumber, builder and koseki-mocksmtpd, none of which anything committed here used.
- Drop the Jeweler and rcov tasks from the Rakefile, keeping spec, spec:lib, spec:plugins and coverage under COVERAGE=on.
- Move the specs to RSpec 3 with rspec-its and rspec-collection_matchers, and load the framework by name rather than by path.
- Skip a plugin spec whose gem is absent, naming the missing gem, instead of aborting the whole suite on a load error.
- Tag the specs that reach real hosts :network and exclude them from the default suite; run them with AUTOMATIC_NETWORK_SPECS=1.
- Stop the FilterFullFeed spec deleting the real ~/.automatic/assets/siteinfo, by redirecting HOME to a temporary directory.
- Replace the FilterRand spec's assertion on one shuffle, which failed at random, with the contract it meant to state.
- Assert that VERSION and Automatic::VERSION agree, rather than asserting a literal version string, and drop the -devel suffix.
- Add specs for Automatic::CLI covering the exit statuses, help, version, an unknown subcommand and a failing recipe.
- Add specs for what Automatic::Recipe accepts and refuses, including a document naming a Ruby class.
- Add .github/workflows/ci.yml running bundle install, a gem build, the CLI and the test suite on Ruby 3.2, 3.3 and 3.4, with no credential configured.
- Rewrite script/build around the current tooling, and stop it running bundle update and unscaffolding a real user directory.
- Remove the references to Jenkins, RubyForge, the git:// clone URL and the automaticruby organization, none of which still resolve.
- Add doc/REQUIREMENTS.md, doc/BASIC_DESIGN.md, doc/POLICY.md, doc/PLUGINS.md, doc/DEPLOYMENT.md, doc/LICENSE.md and this file.
- Specify the Recipe format in doc/PLUGINS.md, including that global.timezone and global.cache are read by nothing and stay that way.
- Specify the plugin contract in doc/PLUGINS.md, to the point where a new plugin can be written from it.
- Catalogue every shipped plugin as Supported, Supported (external), Needs rework or Unsupported, with the reason, and record that a dead plugin is never simulated into passing a test.
- State that a Recipe is trusted local configuration, and that credentials live in it, in the requirements, the design and the policy.
- Rewrite README.md around the current repository, and move the release history from doc/ChangeLog into this file without changing an entry or a date.
- Remove doc/README and doc/PLUGINS, whose content is now in README.md, doc/BASIC_DESIGN.md and doc/PLUGINS.md.
- Keep doc/README.ja and doc/PLUGINS.ja as historical Japanese documentation, marked as describing the software as it was.

